*è⁄¨Í«≤WHÏ“∏Öˆ~v.#ùK6ëc-`∫®{"rendered":{"html":"<p>A future represents a calculation whose result is needed later. <code>lazy</code>\ncreates that calculation without running it. Awaiting it evaluates the\nbody and remembers the successful result. Futures are deferred work, not\nconcurrent tasks.</p>\n<p>This program creates one future and then reads its result:</p>\n<div class=\"code-block\" data-language=\"gcsl\"><button class=\"copy-code\" type=\"button\" data-copy-code><span class=\"copy-label\">Copy</span></button><pre><code>let answer = lazy 21 * 2\nawait answer\n</code></pre></div>\n<h2 id=\"language-deferred-work--where-work-happens\">Where work happens <a class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#language-deferred-work--where-work-happens\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>A <code>lazy</code> body is an impure context, even when a pure function creates it.\nThat lets a pure function plan effects without performing them. A pure\nfunction cannot await a future. Resolve the value in an impure caller\nbefore passing it into a pure calculation that needs the value itself.</p>\n<p>The checker inserts implicit awaits at uses that need a value's contents,\nsuch as arithmetic or a required present argument. It applies the same\npurity restriction to explicit and implicit awaits. A call to a pure\nfunction never secretly starts deferred work inside that function.</p>\n<h2 id=\"language-deferred-work--writing-contracts\">Writing contracts <a class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#language-deferred-work--writing-contracts\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>Use explicit wrappers when the distinction is part of an interface:</p>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\">\n<table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Contract</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><code>Present&lt;Int&gt;</code></td><td>A present integer, equivalent to <code>Int</code> in an ordinary annotation</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>Future&lt;Int&gt;</code></td><td>Deferred work yielding an integer</td></tr>\n<tr><td>`Int</td><td>Future<Int>`</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n<p>A present value does not satisfy an explicitly required <code>Future&lt;T&gt;</code>.\nNested future layers collapse: awaiting a future resolves it fully.\nAwaiting an already present value is a silent no-op.</p>\n<p>Bare types in impure parameters accept present or deferred values,\nincluding bare positions inside records and lists. Pure parameters infer\nwhether their bodies need the value's contents. Impure and memoized\nresults infer their present-or-future shape from the body. Explicit\nwrappers keep the contract you wrote.</p>\n<h2 id=\"language-deferred-work--avoiding-surprising-inference\">Avoiding surprising inference <a class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#language-deferred-work--avoiding-surprising-inference\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>A pure function with result <code>Int</code> requires a present result, even if it\nonly returns its argument. It does not preserve a future's shape\npolymorphically. Recursive result inference also retains conservative\nparts of the declared signature. Use <code>Present&lt;T&gt;</code> or an explicit future\ncontract where an API needs a stable distinction, and consult\n<a href=\"#language-inference-limits\">Inference Limits</a> when an inferred type is wider\nthan expected.</p>\n","toc":[{"level":2,"id":"language-deferred-work--where-work-happens","title":"Where work happens","number":null},{"level":2,"id":"language-deferred-work--writing-contracts","title":"Writing contracts","number":null},{"level":2,"id":"language-deferred-work--avoiding-surprising-inference","title":"Avoiding surprising inference","number":null}],"has_mermaid":false,"dangling":[],"warnings":[],"broken":[]},"images":[],"links":{"pages":[["gcsl/inference-limits",{"Ok":["/gcsl/language/inference-limits","Inference Limits"]}]],"anchors":[]},"refs":null}